```html
<style>
  .box { width: 240px; padding: 16px; border: 2px solid #315b8a; margin: 8px; }
  .content { box-sizing: content-box; }
  .border { box-sizing: border-box; }
</style>
<div class="box content">content-box: visible width is 276px</div>
<div class="box border">border-box: visible width is 240px</div>
```
Inspect both elements in browser Developer Tools. Their declared width matches, but their border-box measurements differ.

```css
html { box-sizing: border-box; }
*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: inherit; }

.field {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 0.75rem;
  border: 2px solid #65758b;
}
```
The field's border edge remains within its parent even though the component has padding and a border.

```html
<style>
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  .row { display: flex; max-width: 24rem; border: 1px solid #65758b; }
  .content { min-width: 0; padding: 1rem; }
  .content a { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
</style>
<div class="row"><div class="content">
  <a href="#">https://example.test/a-very-long-unbroken-address-that-must-wrap</a>
</div></div>
```
Remove min-width: 0 to compare the Flexbox child's default min-content constraint.
